    "https://jobs.tufts.edu/jobs/23770?lang=en-us": {
      "title": "Curriculum Manager in Boston, Massachusetts",
      "description": "Curriculum Manager in Boston, Massachusetts | Tufts University Skip to Main Content toggle menu CLOSE About Benefits Opportunities DEI Community Open Positions Back Curriculum Manager JOB_DESCRIPTION.SHARE.HTML CAROUSEL_PARAGRAPH JOB_DESCRIPTION.SHARE.HTML Boston, Massachusetts Hybrid Academic Programs 23770 mail_outline Get future jobs matching this search Login or Register Job Description Overview The Office of Educational Affairs at Tufts University School of Medicine is primarily responsible for the medical school academic programs, curriculum design and delivery, system-wide evaluation, faculty development, interaction with the offices of Student Affairs, Registrar, and Professional Degree Programs, and promotion of academic excellence through grant participation for innovative programs. What You'll Do The Curriculum Manager is responsible for the day-to-day managing, coordination, and delivery of the pre-clerkship M.D. curriculum at Tufts University School of Medicine. The Curriculum Manager works under the supervision of the Director of Curriculum within the Office of Educational Affairs (OEA) and co-supervises two Curriculum Coordinators, one designated for the 1st year curricular programming and the other designated for the 2nd year curriculum. The Curriculum Manager has responsibility for ensuring the successful delivery of all elements of the first- and second-year curriculum including: creating and editing academic schedules; management of course pages on CANVAS and other learning system platforms; meeting regularly with Course Directors, other faculty, and staff to develop and deliver courses; tracking and managing of educational materials; administration of exams; and interacting with medical students on various aspects related to the pre-clerkship medical curriculum. The Curriculum Manager attends meetings with students, faculty, and other offices within the medical school, such as course director meetings, student representative meetings, and the Curriculum Committee. Manage pre-clerkship (1st and 2nd year) medical school courses: Collaborate with course directors and other OEA staff in the preparation and delivery of each course, including course planning meetings, and monthly course directors’ and curriculum committee meetings. Ensure that course materials (eg, slides, pre-work, recordings) are received from faculty and posted in a timely manner for students Review and edit materials to ensure educational standards are met (eg, learning objectives are appropriate to each session; grading criteria are clear; course hours are compliant). Create and distribute small group assignments for active learning sessions. Interface with Educational Media, Library, CANVAS, and other offices necessary for delivering content. Serve as a resource for students and faculty as questions or problems arise related to any aspect of a course within the pre-clerkship medical curriculum Archive educational materials in accordance with accreditation standards Manage academic schedules for pre-clerkship medical education program. Collaborate with course directors to schedule and confirm courses that abide by regulations set by the Curriculum Committee, considering room and faculty availability and other constraints Enter finalized schedules into learning management systems (LMS) and managing scheduling changes as they arise, including labelling sessions appropriately within each LMS (such as mandatory sessions). Book rooms and educational spaces to hold scheduled sessions Collaborate on activities critical to the mission of the Office of Educational Affairs. Coordinate with the Director of Evaluation and Assessment to ensure exams are developed and delivered in accordance with testing standards. Participate in exam delivery and proctoring as needed. Participate in occasional working groups or task forces coordinated by the Dean of Educational Affairs or Director of Curriculum that are relevant to the pre-clerkship curriculum. Support and contribute to annual updating of OEA policies and manuals. Communicate with other TUSM offices as needed regarding aspects of the pre-clerkship curriculum, such as course start and end dates, exam dates, student enrollment changes, or course hours. Help develop and deliver monthly office “all-in” days and approximately quarterly team-building activities. What We're Looking For Basic Requirements: • Knowledge and skills as typically acquired through Bachelor’s degree • 3+ years of relevant experience in higher education, healthcare, or curriculum delivery • Passion for working with students • Strong organizational skills • Excellent communications skills • Microsoft Office Suite and/or learning management system familiarity Preferred Qualifications: • Experience working with students • Comfort with balancing a range of responsibilities that involve interpersonal and administrative work • Ability to maintain composure, think clearly and alleviate stress of students in challenging situations • Supervisory experience Pay Range Minimum $74,000.00, Midpoint $92,500.00, Maximum $111,000.00 Salary is based on related experience, expertise, and internal equity; generally, new hires can expect pay between the minimum and midpoint of the range. Apply Refer JOB_DESCRIPTION.SHARE.HTML CAROUSEL_PARAGRAPH JOB_DESCRIPTION.SHARE.HTML An employee in this position must complete all appropriate background checks at the time of hire, promotion, or transfer. Tufts University is an equal opportunity employer. Tufts Non-Discrimination Statement Tufts Annual Security Report On Campus Safety Human Resources Office of Equal Opportunity Accessibility Privacy © Tufts University 2026 Cookies are used on this site to assist in continually improving the candidate experience and all the interaction data we store of our visitors is anonymous. Learn more about your rights on our Privacy Policy page. Okay x"
    },
    "https://jobs.tufts.edu/jobs/23787?lang=en-us": {
      "title": "Research Lab IT Manager in Medford, Massachusetts",
      "description": "Research Lab IT Manager in Medford, Massachusetts | Tufts University Skip to Main Content toggle menu CLOSE About Benefits Opportunities DEI Community Open Positions Back Research Lab IT Manager JOB_DESCRIPTION.SHARE.HTML CAROUSEL_PARAGRAPH JOB_DESCRIPTION.SHARE.HTML Medford, Massachusetts Hybrid (IT) Information Technology 23787 mail_outline Get future jobs matching this search Login or Register Job Description Overview The Allen Discovery Center is dedicated to the advancement of basic biology and biomedicine, and to becoming a leader in an integrated approach to the control of biological structure and function. Through a combination of developmental biophysics, computer science, and cognitive science, the work of the Center seeks a multi-level understanding of natural and synthetic living forms. Exploiting novel mechanisms of cellular decision-making, we seek to understand and augment the information processing, computation, and patterning processes that implement the generation and regenerative repair of anatomical structures. Our goal is to complement the mainstream advances focused on the genetically specified hardware of life with new approaches to reprogramming the physiological software inherent in living tissues. Technology is being developed to modulate the bioelectric communication that enables cells to coordinate their activities toward specific anatomical and functional outcomes. Using this bioelectric interface, we develop ways to exploit the collective intelligence of cells toward transformative applications in birth defects, regenerative medicine, cancer reprogramming, and synthetic bioengineering. What You'll Do The Research Lab IT Manager oversees the coordination of the Center's technology services within a research lab environment. This position is responsible for ensuring that the Lab effectively supports, advances the needs, and fulfills the mission of the Center's researchers, faculty, and students.  The Research Lab IT Manager serves as the primary technology partner for the Center, managing and handling large-scale research data, providing expert consultation on complex scientific and technical initiatives, and delivering operational support as needed and when appropriate.  Working closely with Center leadership, the Research Lab IT Manager drives planning efforts to evolve the Center's technology environment and ensures those efforts are implemented effectively.  This includes identifying emerging technologies, recommending best practices, and developing solutions that enable scientific discovery while aligning with institutional requirements. The Research Lab IT Manager also leads the evaluation, selection, and implementation of new hardware and software solutions that meet research needs, including researching available options and optimizing them for specific use cases. This position engages with end users in the Allen Discovery Center, as well as Tufts IT staff, and represents the interests of the Center's researchers in those interactions.  The Research Lab IT Manager acts as the Center's first line of technical support and serves as its advocate within TTS. This includes triaging issues before they are escalated, representing researchers' needs in technology discussions, and independently evaluating proposed solutions to ensure they effectively support the Center's mission and objectives. This position follows central IT governance, standards, service-management practices, security requirements, technical architecture, and established processes. In cases where central frameworks do not meet local needs, the Allen Center IT Manager develops alternate options and reviews those with central IT partners. Consultations Management Serve as the primary technology consultant and trusted advisor to Center leadership, researchers, faculty, staff, and students, providing guidance on technology solutions that support the Center's research mission. Provide expert consultation on complex scientific and technical initiatives, helping researchers identify, evaluate, and implement technology approaches that align with central IT’s ecosystem while advancing research objectives. Collaborate with Center leadership and researchers to assess emerging technology needs and opportunities that support current and future research initiatives. Participate in budget planning activities related to Center equipment and operational resources. Collaborate with central IT to plan and evolve the Center's technology environment by evaluating emerging technologies, industry best practices and innovative solutions. Partner with researchers to understand project requirements and advise on technology strategies from concept through implementation. Represent the interests and technology needs of Center researchers in discussions with Tufts Technology Services and other institutional partners. Evaluate proposed technology solutions to ensure alignment with research priorities, operational requirements, TTS standards, and the Center's mission. Develop and maintain informational resources, documentation, and communications related to Center technology services and initiatives Technology Operations & Asset Management Oversee the day-to-day operation, maintenance, and support of the Allen Discovery Center’s research technology infrastructure, computing systems, and collaboration platforms to enable scientific and operational activities Serve as the primary liaison with TTS, lab vendors, and other university partners regarding space- technology needs. Maintain inventories of Center technology assets, including tagging, tracking, and retirement of hardware, along with documentation and operational standards for Center equipment. Supervise, train, and mentor junior technical staff, student workers, or interns who support the Center’s technology needs. Advise and collaborate with Center stakeholders on technology and operational practices that support research needs while aligning with university policies, safety requirements, and industry best practices. Systems Support Serving as a technology partner to the Center and collaborating closely with researchers and Tufts Technology Services, this position supports the planning, implementation, and operation of technology resources that advance the Center's research mission, including: Manage and maintain the Center's technology environment, including research computing systems, workstations, servers, storage, software, and related infrastructure. Manage the Center's technology infrastructure and shared computing resources, working closely with central IT to align solutions with institutional services while addressing the unique needs of the research environment. Research, evaluate, and recommend hardware, software, and technology services that support scientific discovery and align with institutional requirements. Serve as the Center's first line of technical support, troubleshooting and resolving hardware, software, systems, and connectivity issues affecting research and operational activities. Coordinate user access to Center technology resources, applications, research computing environments, and data resources. Evaluate, implement, configure, and maintain hardware and software solutions that support the Center's research and operational needs. Manage large-scale research data resources and associated data management practices to ensure availability, integrity, and compliance with institutional requirements. Coordinate software licensing, vendor support agreements, technology inventories, and lifecycle management activities. Monitor, test, troubleshoot, and optimize technology systems to ensure reliability, security, and performance. Coordinate technology projects, upgrades, migrations, and lifecycle replacement initiatives. Develop and maintain technical documentation, system standards, operating procedures, and knowledge resources. This is a full-time, primarily on-site position, 35 hours per week over five days. Effective support depends on being physically present with the researchers and their equipment; up to one remote day per week may be arranged. What We're Looking For Basic Requirements: Knowledge and experience typically acquired by: Bachelor's Degree Data Science, Computer Science, or related discipline 5+ years delivering IT support in a research or scientific environment, or an equivalent combination of education and experience Proficiency in operating systems (Windows, MacOS, Linux) and server administration. Knowledge of local area networks, network administration,  network protocols, firewalls, and data security practices. Demonstrated experience in supporting a research-intensive environment, i.e. scientific laboratories, including familiarity with the scientific computing environments and software used there (e.g. MATLAB, R, Python). Strong technical skills in the configuration, installation, and troubleshooting of email and web clients, Antivirus software, TCP/IP, and imaging software as well as experience troubleshooting peripherals (printers, scanners) and handheld devices (mobile, tablets). Working experience with endpoint management and remote support tools, such as Ivanti Endpoint Manager, Microsoft SCCM/MECM, Microsoft Intune, JAMF Pro, or BeyondTrust Remote Support, etc. Strong understanding of emerging AI technologies and the ability to evaluate and recommend their application within the research enterprise, navigating university review and approval processes and considering intellectual property, security, privacy, and other relevant requirements, together with the resourcefulness to use AI tools as a practical aid in diagnosing and resolving unfamiliar, undocumented, or older systems. Strong knowledge of desktop security and standards (security/networking). Significant experience with backup and recovery strategies, WINS, DHCP, DNS, and TCP/IP, and existing products/solutions for backup in a dynamic research environment. Working knowledge of data management best practices for scientific computing, including organizing, archiving, and backing up large research datasets, and verifying that backups can be restored, and evaluating backup products and solutions for a dynamic research environment. Demonstrated ability to independently evaluate technology options and vendors or central-IT proposals, and to advocate effectively on behalf of researchers - including identifying gaps between proposed solutions and research requirements, recommending appropriate alternatives, and clearly communicating trade-offs to non-technical stakeholders. Experience with IT asset lifecycle management, including inventory, tagging, tracking, and retirement of hardware. Experience mentoring, training, or overseeing junior technical staff, student workers, or interns. Experience with configuration, setup, and support of laboratory instrumentation and complex lab functions in a sciences/research setting. Experience supporting legacy or vendor-locked computers attached to laboratory instruments, including systems that cannot be upgraded or patched and must be kept running safely. Experience researching and comparing technology options (storage, hardware, software, services) and presenting clear recommendations and trade-offs to non-IT stakeholders. Strong organizational and attention to detail skills are a vital part of the overall success within this position. Commitment to the Center mission, and flexibility to solve problems in whatever way needed to enable scientists to achieve their goals. Demonstrated excellence in communication and the ability to deliver professional customer service, end user training and consultation, effective team and project work, and to build durable working relationships with colleagues in TTS while still representing the Center’s needs. Preferred Qualifications: A+, Network+, ITIL, or other industry-standard certifications. Pay Range Minimum $108,200.00, Midpoint $135,300.00, Maximum $162,400.00 Salary is based on related experience, expertise, and internal equity; generally, new hires can expect pay between the minimum and midpoint of the range. Apply Refer JOB_DESCRIPTION.SHARE.HTML CAROUSEL_PARAGRAPH JOB_DESCRIPTION.SHARE.HTML An employee in this position must complete all appropriate background checks at the time of hire, promotion, or transfer. Tufts University is an equal opportunity employer. Tufts Non-Discrimination Statement Tufts Annual Security Report On Campus Safety Human Resources Office of Equal Opportunity Accessibility Privacy © Tufts University 2026 Cookies are used on this site to assist in continually improving the candidate experience and all the interaction data we store of our visitors is anonymous. Learn more about your rights on our Privacy Policy page. Okay x"
    },
    "https://jobs.tufts.edu/jobs/23790?lang=en-us": {
      "title": "Dental Hygienist",
      "description": "Dental Hygienist - Tufts Special Care Dental Clinics in Danvers, Massachusetts | Tufts University Skip to Main Content toggle menu CLOSE About Benefits Opportunities DEI Community Open Positions Back Dental Hygienist - Tufts Special Care Dental Clinics JOB_DESCRIPTION.SHARE.HTML CAROUSEL_PARAGRAPH JOB_DESCRIPTION.SHARE.HTML Danvers, Massachusetts On-Site Clinical 23790 mail_outline Get future jobs matching this search Login or Register Job Description Overview This is a part-time position working 21 hours per week, Tuesday-Thursday 8AM - 4PM. This position is located in Danvers, MA. Tufts University School of Dental Medicine (TUSDM) offers one of the most forward-looking educational environments in dental medicine in the country. Tufts Special Care Dental Clinics (formerly Tufts Dental Facilities for Persons with Special Needs) is a network of seven outpatient clinics that provides dental care to approximately 7,000 developmentally disabled patients in the state of Massachusetts under the umbrella of TUSDM and in partnership with the Massachusetts Departments of Public Health (DPH) and Developmental Services (DDS). This position is based at our clinic in Danvers. In addition to outpatient services, patient care is also delivered in operating room settings at three affiliated hospitals within the state. TSCDC serves as a vital teaching venue for General Practice Residents through the Tufts GPR program and for fourth-year dental students, offering hands-on experience in treating patients with special needs. What You'll Do The Dental Hygienist plays a vital role in delivering high quality, patient centered oral health care. This position is responsible for performing comprehensive preventive and therapeutic hygiene services, including clinical assessments, periodontal treatments, radiographs, fluoride applications, and patient education, while ensuring all medical histories and clinical documentation are accurately maintained. Collaborating with the dental team, the Dental Hygienist supports coordinated care, treatment planning, and scheduling to promote optimal oral health outcomes. Through effective communication, clinical expertise, and a commitment to disease prevention, this role contributes to a positive patient experience and the overall effectiveness of dental practice. Review and update medical histories and provide comprehensive preventive and therapeutic oral health care, including assessments, periodontal treatments, fluoride applications, radiographs, and patient/caregiver education. Collaborate with the dental team to support treatment planning and maintain precise detailed clinical documentation, ensuring high quality patient care. Support clinical operations by managing hygiene and clinical supplies, maintaining equipment and infection control protocols, ensuring OSHA and HIPAA compliance, and providing backup assistance with appointment confirmations and scheduling. Promote smooth clinic operations and a positive patient experience by collaborating with staff and trainees, maintaining a professional and welcoming environment, and demonstrating patient centered values that support the clinic’s mission. All other duties as required. Travel to other clinics is required. What We're Looking For Basic Requirements: Knowledge and experience typically acquired by: Associate's Degree in Dental Hygiene. 1-3 years Clinical dental hygiene experience. Intermediate proficiency in dental practice management software and Microsoft Office applications. Massachusetts Dental Hygiene License. Travel to other Tufts Special Care Dental Clinics is required. Preferred Qualifications: Bachelor's Degree. Experience in treating patients with developmental, behavioral, or medical complexities. Pay Range Minimum $49.30, Midpoint $58.70, Maximum $68.10 Salary is based on related experience, expertise, and internal equity; generally, new hires can expect pay between the minimum and midpoint of the range. Apply Refer JOB_DESCRIPTION.SHARE.HTML CAROUSEL_PARAGRAPH JOB_DESCRIPTION.SHARE.HTML An employee in this position must complete all appropriate background checks at the time of hire, promotion, or transfer. Tufts University is an equal opportunity employer. Tufts Non-Discrimination Statement Tufts Annual Security Report On Campus Safety Human Resources Office of Equal Opportunity Accessibility Privacy © Tufts University 2026 Cookies are used on this site to assist in continually improving the candidate experience and all the interaction data we store of our visitors is anonymous. Learn more about your rights on our Privacy Policy page. Okay x"
    },
    "https://jobs.tufts.edu/jobs/23789?lang=en-us": {
      "title": "Dental Hygienist",
